        "*Your answer:*\n",
        "> False Positives are more costly in a real spam filter because they cause legitimate messages to be incorrectly marked as spam. For example, an important message from a bank, university, or employer could be blocked and never reach the user. False Negatives also create problems because spam messages can reach the user's inbox, potentially exposing them to phishing links, scams, or malicious content. However, blocking important legitimate messages can have serious consequences, so I would consider False Positives more costly.\n",
